## sn12.66
sn12.66:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn12.66:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kurus, near the Kuru town named Kammāsadamma. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Kurus, where there was a town of the Kurus named Kammasadamma.
sn12.66:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn12.66:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn12.66:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn12.66:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn12.66:1.7 | S: “Mendicants, do you probe within?” | T: “Do you engage in inward exploration, bhikkhus?”
sn12.66:1.8 | S: When he said this, one of the mendicants said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, one bhikkhu said to the Blessed One:
sn12.66:1.9 | S: “Sir, I probe within.” | T: “Venerable sir, I engage in inward exploration.”
sn12.66:1.10 | S: “But mendicant, how do you probe within?” | T: “How do you engage in inward exploration, bhikkhu?”
sn12.66:1.11 | S: Then that mendicant answered, | T: The bhikkhu then explained
sn12.66:1.12 | S: but the Buddha was not happy with the answer. | T: but the way he explained did not satisfy the Blessed One.
sn12.66:2.1 | S: When he had spoken, Venerable Ānanda said to the Buddha, | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda said:
sn12.66:2.2 | S: “Now is the time, Blessed One! Now is the time, Holy One! | T: “Now is the time for this, Blessed One! Now is the time for this, Fortunate One!
sn12.66:2.3 | S: Let the Buddha speak of probing within. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: Let the Blessed One explain inward exploration. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the bhikkhus will remember it.”
sn12.66:2.4 | S: “Well then, Ānanda, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then listen and attend closely, Ānanda, I will speak.”
sn12.66:2.5 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” the bhikkhus replied.
sn12.66:2.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn12.66:3.1 | S: “Take a mendicant who probes within: | T: “Here, bhikkhus, when engaged in inward exploration, a bhikkhu explores thus:
sn12.66:3.2 | S: ‘The suffering that arises in the world starting with old age and death takes many and diverse forms. | T: ‘The many diverse kinds of suffering that arise in the world headed by aging-and-death:
sn12.66:3.3 | S: But what is the source of this suffering? When what exists do old age and death come to be? And when what does not exist do old age and death not come to be?’ | T: what is the source of this suffering, what is its origin, from what is it born and produced? When what exists does aging-and-death come to be? When what does not exist does aging-and-death not come to be?’
sn12.66:3.4 | S: While probing they know: | T: “As he explores he understands thus:
sn12.66:3.5 | S: ‘The suffering that arises in the world starting with old age and death takes many and diverse forms. | T: ‘The many diverse kinds of suffering that arise in the world headed by aging-and-death:
sn12.66:3.6 | S: The source of this suffering is attachment. When attachments exist old age and death come to be. And when attachments do not exist old age and death don’t come to be.’ | T: this suffering has acquisition as its source, acquisition as its origin; it is born and produced from acquisition. When there is acquisition, aging-and-death comes to be; when there is no acquisition, aging-and-death does not come to be.’
sn12.66:3.7 | S: They understand old age and death, their origin, their cessation, and the fitting practice for their cessation. | T: “He understands aging-and-death, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading on that is in conformity with its cessation.
sn12.66:3.8 | S: And they practice in line with that path. | T: He practises in that way and conducts himself accordingly.
sn12.66:3.9 | S: This is called a mendicant who is practicing for the complete ending of suffering, for the cessation of old age and death. | T: This is called a bhikkhu who is practising for the utterly complete destruction of suffering, for the cessation of aging-and-death.
sn12.66:4.1 | S: They probe further within: | T: “Then, engaging further in inward exploration, he explores thus:
sn12.66:4.2 | S: ‘But what is the source of this attachment? When what exists does attachment come to be? And when what does not exist does attachment not come to be?’ | T: ‘What is the source of this acquisition, what is its origin, from what is it born and produced? When what exists does acquisition come to be? When what is absent does acquisition not come to be?’
sn12.66:4.3 | S: While probing they know: | T: “As he explores he understands thus:
sn12.66:4.4 | S: ‘The source of this attachment is craving. When craving exists attachments come to be. And when craving doesn’t exist attachments don’t come to be.’ | T: ‘Acquisition has craving as its source, craving as its origin; it is born and produced from craving. When there is craving, acquisition comes to be; when there is no craving, acquisition does not come to be.’
sn12.66:4.5 | S: They understand attachments, their origin, their cessation, and the fitting practice for their cessation. | T: “He understands acquisition, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading on that is in conformity with its cessation.
sn12.66:4.6 | S: And they practice in line with that path. | T: He practises in that way and conducts himself accordingly.
sn12.66:4.7 | S: This is called a mendicant who is practicing for the complete ending of suffering, for the cessation of attachments. | T: This is called a bhikkhu who is practising for the utterly complete destruction of suffering, for the cessation of acquisition.
sn12.66:5.1 | S: They probe further within: | T: “Then, engaging further in inward exploration, he explores thus:
sn12.66:5.2 | S: ‘But where does that craving arise and where does it settle?’ | T: ‘When this craving arises, where does it arise? When it settles down, upon what does it settle?’
sn12.66:5.3 | S: While probing they know: | T: “As he explores he understands thus:
sn12.66:5.4 | S: ‘That craving arises and settles on whatever in the world seems nice and pleasant. | T: ‘Whatever in the world has a pleasant and agreeable nature: it is here that this craving arises when it arises; it is here that it settles when it settles down.’
sn12.66:5.5 | S: And what in the world seems nice and pleasant? | T: And what in the world has a pleasant and agreeable nature?
sn12.66:5.6 | S: The eye in the world seems nice and pleasant, and it is there that craving arises and settles. | T: The eye has a pleasant and agreeable nature in the world: it is here that this craving arises when it arises; it is here that it settles when it settles down.
sn12.66:5.7 | S: The ear … | T: So too the ear,
sn12.66:5.8 | S: nose … | T: the nose,
sn12.66:5.9 | S: tongue … | T: the tongue,
sn12.66:5.10 | S: body … | T: the body,
sn12.66:5.11 | S: mind in the world seems nice and pleasant, and it is there that craving arises and settles.’ | T: and the mind have a pleasant and agreeable nature: it is here that this craving arises when it arises; it is here that it settles when it settles down.
sn12.66:6.1 | S: There were ascetics and brahmins of the past who saw the things that seem nice and pleasant in the world as permanent, as pleasurable, as self, as healthy, and as safe. | T: “Bhikkhus, whatever ascetics and brahmins in the past regarded that in the world with a pleasant and agreeable nature as permanent, as happiness, as self, as healthy, as secure:
sn12.66:6.2 | S: Their craving grew. | T: they nurtured craving.
sn12.66:6.3 | S: As their craving grew, their attachments grew. | T: In nurturing craving they nurtured acquisition.
sn12.66:6.4 | S: As their attachments grew, their suffering grew. | T: In nurturing acquisition they nurtured suffering.
sn12.66:6.5 | S: And as their suffering grew, they were not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They were not freed from suffering, I say. | T: In nurturing suffering they were not freed from birth, aging, and death; they were not freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair; they were not freed from suffering, I say.
