## an3.40
an3.40:1.1 | S: “There are, mendicants, these three things to put in charge. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three authorities.
an3.40:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.40:1.3 | S: Putting oneself, the world, or the teaching in charge. | T: Oneself as one’s authority, the world as one’s authority, and the Dhamma as one’s authority.
an3.40:1.4 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting oneself in charge? | T: (1) “And what, bhikkhus, is oneself as one’s authority?
an3.40:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an3.40:1.6 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘I did not go forth from the household life into homelessness for the sake of a robe,
an3.40:1.7 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: almsfood, or lodging, or for the sake of becoming this or that,
an3.40:1.8 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death; by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: but rather with the thought: “I am immersed in birth, old age, and death; in sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection, and anguish. I am immersed in suffering, afflicted by suffering.
an3.40:1.9 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: Perhaps an ending of this entire mass of suffering can be discerned.”
an3.40:1.10 | S: But it would not be appropriate for me to seek sensual pleasures like those I abandoned when I went forth, or even worse.’ | T: As one who has gone forth from the household life into homelessness, it would not be proper for me to seek out sensual pleasures similar to or worse than those that I have discarded.’
an3.40:1.11 | S: Then they reflect: | T: He then reflects thus:
an3.40:1.12 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘Energy will be aroused in me without slackening; mindfulness will be established without confusion; my body will be tranquil without disturbance; my mind will be concentrated and one-pointed.’
an3.40:1.13 | S: Putting themselves in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having taken himself as his authority, he abandons the unwholesome and develops the wholesome; he abandons what is blameworthy and develops what is blameless; he maintains himself in purity.
an3.40:1.14 | S: This is called putting oneself in charge. | T: This is called oneself as one’s authority.
an3.40:2.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting the world in charge? | T: (2) “And what, bhikkhus, is the world as one’s authority?
an3.40:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an3.40:2.3 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘I did not go forth from the household life into homelessness for the sake of a robe …
an3.40:2.4 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: (none)
an3.40:2.5 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death, by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: but rather with the thought: “I am immersed in birth, old age, and death …
an3.40:2.6 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: Perhaps an ending of this entire mass of suffering can be discerned.”
an3.40:2.7 | S: And now, since I’ve now gone forth, I might have sensual, malicious, or cruel thoughts. But the population of the world is large, | T: As one who has gone forth from the household life into homelessness, I might think sensual thoughts, thoughts of ill will, or thoughts of harming. But the abode of the world is vast.
an3.40:2.8 | S: and there are ascetics and brahmins who have psychic power—they’re clairvoyant, and can read the minds of others. | T: In the vast abode of the world there are ascetics and brahmins with psychic potency and the divine eye who know the minds of others.
an3.40:2.9 | S: They see far without being seen, even by those close; and they understand the minds of others. | T: They see things from a distance but they are not themselves seen even when they’re close; they know the minds of others with their own mind.
an3.40:2.10 | S: They would know me: | T: They would know me thus:
an3.40:2.11 | S: “Look at this gentleman; he’s gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, but he’s living mixed up with bad, unskillful qualities.” | T: “Look at this clansman: though he has gone forth from the household life into homelessness out of faith, he is tarnished by bad unwholesome states.”
an3.40:2.12 | S: And there are deities, too, who have psychic power—they’re clairvoyant, and can read the minds of others. | T: There are deities, too, with psychic potency and the divine eye who know the minds of others.
an3.40:2.13 | S: They see far without being seen, even by those close; and they understand the minds of others. | T: They see even from a distance but are not seen themselves even when close; they too know the minds of others with their own mind.
an3.40:2.14 | S: They would know me: | T: They too would know me thus:
an3.40:2.15 | S: “Look at this gentleman; he’s gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, but he’s living mixed up with bad, unskillful qualities.”’ | T: “Look at this clansman: though he has gone forth from the household life into homelessness out of faith, he is tarnished by bad unwholesome states.”’
an3.40:2.16 | S: Then they reflect: | T: He then reflects thus:
an3.40:2.17 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘Energy will be aroused in me without slackening; mindfulness will be established without confusion; my body will be tranquil without disturbance; my mind will be concentrated and one-pointed.’
an3.40:2.18 | S: Putting the world in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having taken the world as his authority, he abandons the unwholesome and develops the wholesome; he abandons what is blameworthy and develops what is blameless; he maintains himself in purity.
an3.40:2.19 | S: This is called putting the world in charge. | T: This is called the world as one’s authority.
an3.40:3.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting the teaching in charge? | T: (3) “And what, bhikkhus, is the Dhamma as one’s authority?
an3.40:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an3.40:3.3 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘I did not go forth from the household life into homelessness for the sake of a robe …
an3.40:3.4 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: (none)
an3.40:3.5 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death, by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: but rather with the thought: “I am immersed in birth, old age, and death …
an3.40:3.6 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: Perhaps an ending of this entire mass of suffering can be discerned.”
an3.40:3.7 | S: The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves. | T: The Dhamma is well expounded by the Blessed One, directly visible, immediate, inviting one to come and see, applicable, to be personally experienced by the wise.
an3.40:3.8 | S: I have spiritual companions who live knowing and seeing. | T: There are fellow monks of mine who know and see.
an3.40:3.9 | S: Now that I’ve gone forth in this well explained teaching and training, it would not be appropriate for me to live lazy and heedless.’ | T: As one who has gone forth from the household life into homelessness in this well-expounded Dhamma and discipline, it would be improper for me to be lazy and heedless.’
an3.40:3.10 | S: Then they reflect: | T: He then reflects thus:
an3.40:3.11 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘Energy will be aroused in me without slackening; mindfulness will be established without confusion; my body will be tranquil without disturbance; my mind will be concentrated and one-pointed.’
an3.40:3.12 | S: Putting the teaching in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having taken the Dhamma as his authority, he abandons the unwholesome and develops the wholesome; he abandons what is blameworthy and develops what is blameless; he maintains himself in purity.
an3.40:3.13 | S: This is called putting the teaching in charge. | T: This is called the Dhamma as one’s authority.
an3.40:3.14 | S: These are the three things to put in charge. | T: “These, bhikkhus, are the three authorities.”
an3.40:4.1 | S: There’s no privacy in the world, | T: For one performing an evil deed there is no place in the world called “hidden.”
an3.40:4.2 | S: for someone who does bad deeds. | T: (none)
an3.40:4.3 | S: You’ll know for yourself, | T: The self within you knows, O person,
an3.40:4.4 | S: whether you’ve lied or told the truth. | T: whether it is true or false.
an3.40:5.1 | S: When you witness your good self, | T: Indeed, sir, you the witness
an3.40:5.2 | S: you despise it; | T: despise your good self;
an3.40:5.3 | S: while you disguise | T: (none)
an3.40:5.4 | S: your bad self inside yourself. | T: you conceal the evil self existing within yourself.
an3.40:6.1 | S: The gods and the Realized One see | T: The devas and Tathāgatas see the fool
an3.40:6.2 | S: the fool who lives unjustly in the world. | T: acting unrighteously in the world.
an3.40:6.3 | S: So with yourself in charge, live mindfully; | T: Therefore one should fare mindfully, taking oneself as authority;
an3.40:6.4 | S: with the world in charge, <j>be alert and practice absorption; | T: alert and meditative, taking the world as authority;
an3.40:6.5 | S: with the teaching in charge, <j>live in line with that teaching: | T: and fare in accordance with the Dhamma, taking the Dhamma as authority.
an3.40:6.6 | S: a sage who tries for the truth doesn’t deteriorate. | T: Truly exerting himself, a sage does not decline.
an3.40:7.1 | S: Māra’s conquered; the terminator’s overcome: | T: Having vanquished Māra and overcome the end-maker,
an3.40:7.2 | S: one who strives reaches the end of rebirth. | T: the striver has finished with birth.

