## an4.33
an4.33:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, toward evening the lion, king of beasts, emerges from his den, | T: “Bhikkhus, in the evening the lion, the king of beasts, comes out from his lair,
an4.33:1.2 | S: yawns, | T: stretches his body,
an4.33:1.3 | S: surveys the four quarters, | T: surveys the four quarters all around,
an4.33:1.4 | S: and roars his lion’s roar three times. | T: and roars his lion’s roar three times.
an4.33:1.5 | S: Then he sets out on the hunt. | T: Then he sets out in search of game.
an4.33:1.6 | S: And the animals who hear the roar of the lion, king of beasts, are typically filled with fear, awe, and terror. | T: “Whatever animals hear the lion roaring for the most part are filled with fear, a sense of urgency, and terror.
an4.33:1.7 | S: They return to their lairs, be they in a hole, the water, or a wood; and the birds take to the air. | T: Those who live in holes enter their holes; those who live in the water enter the water; those who live in the woods enter the woods; and the birds resort to the sky.
an4.33:1.8 | S: Even the royal elephants, bound with strong harnesses in the villages, towns, and capital cities, break apart their bonds, and urinate and defecate in terror as they flee here and there. | T: Even those royal bull elephants, bound by strong thongs in the villages, towns, and capital cities, burst and break their bonds asunder; frightened, they urinate and defecate and flee here and there.
an4.33:1.9 | S: That’s how powerful is the lion, king of beasts, over animals, how illustrious and mighty. | T: So powerful among the animals is the lion, the king of beasts, so majestic and mighty.
an4.33:2.1 | S: In the same way, when a Realized One arises in the world—perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed—he teaches the Dhamma: | T: “So too, bhikkhus, when the Tathāgata arises in the world, an arahant, perfectly enlightened, accomplished in true knowledge and conduct, fortunate, knower of the world, unsurpassed trainer of persons to be tamed, teacher of devas and humans, the Enlightened One, the Blessed One, he teaches the Dhamma thus:
an4.33:2.2 | S: ‘Such is substantial reality, such is the origin of substantial reality, such is the cessation of substantial reality, such is the practice that leads to the cessation of substantial reality.’ | T: ‘(1) Such is personal existence, (2) such the origin of personal existence, (3) such the cessation of personal existence, (4) such the way to the cessation of personal existence.’
an4.33:2.3 | S: Now, there are gods who are long-lived, beautiful, and very happy, lasting long in their divine palaces. When they hear this teaching by the Realized One, they’re typically filled with fear, awe, and terror. | T: “When those devas who are long-lived, beautiful, abounding in happiness, dwelling for a long time in lofty palaces, hear the Tathāgata’s teaching of the Dhamma, for the most part they are filled with fear, a sense of urgency, and terror thus:
an4.33:2.4 | S: ‘Oh no! It turns out we’re impermanent, though we thought we were permanent! | T: ‘It seems that we are actually impermanent, though we thought ourselves permanent;
an4.33:2.5 | S: It turns out we don’t last, though we thought we were everlasting! | T: it seems that we are actually transient, though we thought ourselves everlasting;
an4.33:2.6 | S: It turns out we’re transient, though we thought we were eternal! | T: it seems that we are actually non-eternal, though we thought ourselves eternal.
an4.33:2.7 | S: It turns out that we’re impermanent, not lasting, transient, and included within substantial reality.’ | T: It seems that we are impermanent, transient, non-eternal, included in personal existence.’
an4.33:2.8 | S: That’s how powerful is the Realized One in the world with its gods, how illustrious and mighty. | T: So powerful is the Tathāgata, so majestic and mighty is he in this world together with its devas.”
an4.33:3.1 | S: The Buddha, the unrivaled Teacher | T: When, through direct knowledge, the Buddha, the teacher, the peerless person
an4.33:3.2 | S: in all the world with its gods, | T: in this world with its devas,
an4.33:3.3 | S: rolls forth the Wheel of Dhamma | T: sets in motion the wheel of Dhamma,
an4.33:3.4 | S: from his own insight: | T: (none)
an4.33:4.1 | S: substantial reality, its cessation, | T: he teaches personal existence, its cessation,
an4.33:4.2 | S: the origin of substantial reality, | T: the origin of personal existence,
an4.33:4.3 | S: and the noble eightfold path | T: and the noble eightfold path
an4.33:4.4 | S: that leads to the stilling of suffering. | T: that leads to the calming down of suffering.
an4.33:5.1 | S: And then the long-lived gods, | T: Then even those devas with long life spans—
an4.33:5.2 | S: so beautiful and glorious, | T: beautiful, ablaze with glory—
an4.33:5.3 | S: are afraid and full of terror, | T: become fearful and filled with terror,
an4.33:5.4 | S: like the other beasts when they hear a lion. | T: like beasts who hear the lion’s roar.
an4.33:6.1 | S: ‘We haven’t passed beyond substantial reality! | T: “It seems that we are impermanent, not beyond personal existence,”
an4.33:6.2 | S: It turns out we’re impermanent!’ | T: (none)
an4.33:6.3 | S: So they say when they hear the word | T: they say, when they hear the word of the Arahant,
an4.33:6.4 | S: of the perfected one, free and unaffected.” | T: the Stable One who is fully freed.
an4.33:6.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.76
sn22.76:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.76:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, form is impermanent. | T: “Bhikkhus, form is impermanent.
sn22.76:1.3 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: What is impermanent is suffering.
sn22.76:1.4 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: What is suffering is nonself.
sn22.76:1.5 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: What is nonself should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.76:1.6 | S: Feeling … | T: “Feeling is impermanent….
sn22.76:1.7 | S: Perception … | T: Perception is impermanent….
sn22.76:1.8 | S: Choices … | T: Volitional formations are impermanent….
sn22.76:1.9 | S: Consciousness is impermanent. | T: Consciousness is impermanent.
sn22.76:1.10 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: What is impermanent is suffering.
sn22.76:1.11 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: What is suffering is nonself.
sn22.76:1.12 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: What is nonself should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.76:2.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple experiences revulsion towards form, revulsion towards feeling, revulsion towards perception, revulsion towards volitional formations, revulsion towards consciousness.
sn22.76:2.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Experiencing revulsion, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion his mind is liberated. When it is liberated there comes the knowledge: ‘It’s liberated.’
sn22.76:2.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He understands: ‘Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.’
sn22.76:2.4 | S: As far as there are abodes of sentient beings, even up until the pinnacle of existence, the perfected ones are the foremost and the best.” | T: “To whatever extent, bhikkhus, there are abodes of beings, even up to the pinnacle of existence, these are the foremost in the world, these are the best, that is, the arahants.”
sn22.76:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn22.76:3.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn22.76:4.1 | S: “Oh! How happy are the perfected ones! | T: “Happy indeed are the arahants!
sn22.76:4.2 | S: Craving is not found in them, | T: No craving can be found in them.
sn22.76:4.3 | S: the conceit ‘I am’ is cut off, | T: Cut off is the conceit ‘I am,’
sn22.76:4.4 | S: and the net of delusion is shattered. | T: Burst asunder is delusion’s net.
sn22.76:5.1 | S: They’ve attained imperturbability, | T: (none)
sn22.76:5.2 | S: their minds are unclouded, | T: Limpid are their minds;
sn22.76:5.3 | S: nothing in the world clings to them, | T: They are unsullied in the world—
sn22.76:5.4 | S: manifesting divinity, undefiled. | T: The holy ones, without taints.
sn22.76:6.1 | S: Completely understanding the five aggregates, | T: “Having fully understood the five aggregates,
sn22.76:6.2 | S: their domain is the seven good qualities. | T: Ranging in the seven good qualities,
sn22.76:6.3 | S: Those true persons are praiseworthy, | T: Those praiseworthy superior men
sn22.76:6.4 | S: the Buddha’s bosom sons. | T: Are the Buddha’s bosom sons.
sn22.76:7.1 | S: Endowed with the seven gems, | T: “Endowed with the seven gems,
sn22.76:7.2 | S: and trained in the three trainings, | T: Trained in the threefold training,
sn22.76:7.3 | S: the great heroes live on, | T: Those great heroes wander about
sn22.76:7.4 | S: with fear and dread given up. | T: With fear and trembling abandoned.
sn22.76:8.1 | S: Endowed with ten factors, | T: “Endowed with the ten factors,
sn22.76:8.2 | S: those giants are immersed in samādhi. | T: Those great nagas, concentrated,
sn22.76:8.3 | S: They are the best in the world, | T: Are the best beings in the world:
sn22.76:8.4 | S: craving is not found in them. | T: No craving can be found in them.
sn22.76:9.1 | S: The adept’s knowledge has arisen: | T: “The adepts’ knowledge has arisen in them:
sn22.76:9.2 | S: ‘This bag of bones is my last.’ | T: ‘This body is the last I bear.’
sn22.76:9.3 | S: They are independent of others | T: (none)
sn22.76:9.4 | S: in the core of the spiritual path. | T: In regard to the core of the holy life They no longer depend on others.
sn22.76:10.1 | S: They do not waver due to the discriminations, | T: (none)
sn22.76:10.2 | S: they’re freed from future lives. | T: They are released from renewed existence.
sn22.76:10.3 | S: They’ve reached the level of the tamed, | T: Having reached the stage of the tamed,
sn22.76:10.4 | S: in the world, they’re the winners. | T: They are the victors in the world.
sn22.76:11.1 | S: Above, below, all round, | T: “Above, across, and below,
sn22.76:11.2 | S: relishing is not found in them. | T: Delight is no more found in them.
sn22.76:11.3 | S: They roar their lion’s roar: | T: They boldly sound their lion’s roar:
sn22.76:11.4 | S: ‘The awakened are supreme in the world!’” | T: ‘The enlightened are supreme in the world.’”
sn22.76:11.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.136
sn35.136:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, gods and humans like sights, they love them and enjoy them. | T: “Bhikkhus, devas and humans delight in forms, take delight in forms, rejoice in forms.
sn35.136:1.2 | S: But when sights perish, fade away, and cease, gods and humans live in suffering. | T: With the change, fading away, and cessation of forms, devas and humans dwell in suffering.
sn35.136:1.3 | S: Gods and humans like sounds … | T: Devas and humans delight in sounds …
sn35.136:1.4 | P: Saddavipariṇāmavirāganirodhā dukkhā, bhikkhave, devamanussā viharanti. | T: (none)
sn35.136:1.5 | S: smells … | T: delight in odours …
sn35.136:1.6 | S: tastes … | T: delight in tastes …
sn35.136:1.7 | S: touches … | T: delight in tactile objects …
sn35.136:1.8 | S: ideas, they love them and enjoy them. | T: delight in mental phenomena, take delight in mental phenomena, rejoice in mental phenomena.
sn35.136:1.9 | S: But when ideas perish, fade away, and cease, gods and humans live in suffering. | T: With the change, fading away, and cessation of mental phenomena, devas and humans dwell in suffering.
sn35.136:1.10 | S: The Realized One has truly understood the origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape of sights, so he doesn’t like, love, or enjoy them. | T: “But, bhikkhus, the Tathagata, the Arahant, the Fully Enlightened One, has understood as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of forms. He does not delight in forms, does not take delight in forms, does not rejoice in forms.
sn35.136:1.11 | S: When sights perish, fade away, and cease, the Realized One lives happily. | T: With the change, fading away, and cessation of forms, the Tathagata dwells happily.
sn35.136:1.12 | S: The Realized One has truly understood the origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape of sounds … | T: “He has understood as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of sounds …
sn35.136:1.13 | S: smells … | T: odours …
sn35.136:1.14 | S: tastes … | T: tastes …
sn35.136:1.15 | S: touches … | T: tactile objects …
sn35.136:1.16 | S: ideas, so he doesn’t like, love, or enjoy them. | T: mental phenomena. He does not delight in mental phenomena, does not take delight in mental phenomena, does not rejoice in mental phenomena.
sn35.136:1.17 | S: When ideas perish, fade away, and cease, the Realized One lives happily.” | T: With the change, fading away, and cessation of mental phenomena, the Tathagata dwells happily.”
sn35.136:1.18 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn35.136:1.19 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn35.136:2.1 | S: “Sights, sounds, tastes, smells, | T: “Forms, sounds, odours, tastes,
sn35.136:2.2 | S: touches and ideas, the lot of them— | T: Tactiles and all objects of mind—
sn35.136:2.3 | S: they’re likable, desirable, and pleasurable | T: Desirable, lovely, agreeable,
sn35.136:2.4 | S: as long as you can say that they exist. | T: So long as it’s said: ‘They are.’
sn35.136:3.1 | S: For all the world with its gods, | T: “These are considered happiness By the world with its devas;
sn35.136:3.2 | S: this is what they deem happiness. | T: (none)
sn35.136:3.3 | S: And where they cease | T: But where these cease,
sn35.136:3.4 | S: is deemed as suffering for them. | T: That they consider suffering.
sn35.136:4.1 | S: The noble ones have seen that happiness | T: “The noble ones have seen as happiness
sn35.136:4.2 | S: is the cessation of substantial reality. | T: The ceasing of identity.
sn35.136:4.3 | S: This insight by those who see | T: This view of those who clearly see
sn35.136:4.4 | S: contradicts the whole world. | T: Runs counter to the entire world.
sn35.136:5.1 | S: What others say is happiness | T: “What others speak of as happiness,
sn35.136:5.2 | S: the noble ones say is suffering. | T: That the noble ones say is suffering;
sn35.136:5.3 | S: What others say is suffering | T: What others speak of as suffering,
sn35.136:5.4 | S: the noble ones know as happiness. | T: That the noble ones know as bliss.
sn35.136:6.1 | S: See, this teaching is hard to understand, | T: “Behold this Dhamma hard to comprehend:
sn35.136:6.2 | S: it confuses the ignorant. | T: Here the foolish are bewildered.
sn35.136:6.3 | S: There is darkness for the shrouded; | T: For those with blocked minds it is obscure,
sn35.136:6.4 | S: blackness for those who don’t see. | T: Sheer darkness for those who do not see.
sn35.136:7.1 | S: But the good are open; | T: “But for the good it is disclosed,
sn35.136:7.2 | S: like light for those who see. | T: It is light here for those who see.
sn35.136:7.3 | S: Though close, they do not understand, | T: The dullards unskilled in the Dhamma Don’t understand it in its presence.
sn35.136:7.4 | S: the seekers inexpert in the teaching. | T: (none)
sn35.136:8.1 | S: They’re mired in desire for continued existence, | T: “This Dhamma isn’t easily understood By those afflicted with lust for existence,
sn35.136:8.2 | S: flowing along the stream of lives, | T: Who flow along in the stream of existence,
sn35.136:8.3 | S: mired in Māra’s dominion: | T: Deeply mired in Mara’s realm.
sn35.136:8.4 | S: this teaching isn’t easy for them to understand. | T: (none)
sn35.136:9.1 | S: Who, apart from the noble ones, | T: “Who else apart from the noble ones
sn35.136:9.2 | S: is qualified to understand this state? | T: Are able to understand this state?
sn35.136:9.3 | S: Having rightly understood this state, | T: When they have rightly known that state,
sn35.136:9.4 | S: the undefiled are fully extinguished.” | T: The taintless ones are fully quenched.”
sn35.136:9.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.39
an10.39:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
an10.39:1.2 | S: “Sir, they speak of ‘schism in the Saṅgha’. | T: “Bhante, it is said: ‘Schism in the Saṅgha, schism in the Saṅgha.’
an10.39:1.3 | S: How is schism in the Saṅgha defined?” | T: How is there schism in the Saṅgha?”
an10.39:1.4 | S: “Ānanda, it’s when a mendicant explains what is not the teaching as the teaching … | T: “Here, Ānanda, (1) bhikkhus explain non-Dhamma as Dhamma … as in AN10.37 …
an10.39:1.5 | S: and what was prescribed by the Realized One as not prescribed by the Realized One. | T: and (10) what has been prescribed by the Tathāgata as not having been prescribed by him.
an10.39:1.6 | S: On these ten grounds they split off and go their own way. They perform legal acts autonomously and recite the monastic code autonomously. | T: On these ten grounds they withdraw and go apart. They perform legal acts separately and recite the Pātimokkha separately.
an10.39:1.7 | S: That is how schism in the Saṅgha is defined.” | T: It is in this way, Ānanda, that there is schism in the Saṅgha.”
an10.39:2.1 | S: “But sir, what does someone who has split a harmonious Saṅgha brim with?” | T: “But, Bhante, when one causes schism in a harmonious Saṅgha, what does one generate?”
an10.39:2.2 | S: “They brim with sin that lasts for an eon.” | T: “One generates evil lasting for an eon, Ānanda.”
an10.39:2.3 | S: “But sir, what is the sin that lasts for an eon?” | T: “But, Bhante, what is that evil lasting for an eon?”
an10.39:2.4 | S: “They burn in hell for an eon, Ānanda. | T: “One is tormented in hell for an eon, Ānanda.”
an10.39:3.1 | S: A schismatic remains for the eon | T: One who causes schism in the Saṅgha is bound for misery, bound for hell, to abide there for an eon.
an10.39:3.2 | S: in a place of loss, in hell. | T: (none)
an10.39:3.3 | S: Taking a stand against the teaching, | T: Delighting in factions, established in non-Dhamma,
an10.39:3.4 | S: favoring factions, they ruin their sanctuary. | T: he falls away from security from bondage.
an10.39:3.5 | S: After causing schism in a harmonious Saṅgha, | T: Having caused schism in a harmonious Saṅgha,
an10.39:3.6 | S: they burn in hell for an eon.” | T: he is tormented in hell for an eon.
an10.39:3.7 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


